  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>&nbsp;of copper gave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of its oxide means,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of oxygen combine with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of copper.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8757;</mo></math> Eq. wt of oxygen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math>,</p><p>Therefore,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of oxygen combine with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of copper <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math></p><p>Hence,&nbsp;</p><p>Equivalent weight of copper <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></math></p>"
